diff --git a/docs/bots.md b/docs/bots.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..edc9203
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/bots.md
@@ -0,0 +1,58 @@
+# Chat bots
+
+A bot is a room member that posts messages through an HTTP API instead of a browser, which
+makes it useful from shell scripts and CI jobs.
+
+## Using the API
+
+A bot posts a message by sending its message body as the raw request body to a room:
+
+```sh
+curl -d "Deploy of v1.2.3 finished ✓" \
+  https://chat.example.com/rooms/<room-id>/<bot-code>/messages
+```
+
+There is no envelope and no field name: whatever the request body is becomes the message
+body. An uploaded file in a `multipart/form-data` request is attached to the message under
+any field name, since bots aren't told what to call it.
+
+The responses are:
+
+| Status | When |
+| --- | --- |
+| `201 Created` | With a `Location` header pointing at `/messages/:message_id` |
+| `422 Unprocessable Content` | The request had neither a body nor an attachment |
+| `404 Not Found` | The room id doesn't exist |
+| `401 Unauthorized` | The bot code doesn't match a bot |
+
+## How bots are modelled
+
+Bots are single table inheritance subclasses of `User`, stored in the `users` table with a
+`type` of `"Bot"`. That's what makes them first-class chat members:
+
+- A new bot joins every open room, because `User` grants membership to open rooms on create.
+- They can be added to closed rooms and mentioned like a person can, since room memberships
+  and mentions both point at users.
+- Their messages have a `creator`, so they're rendered, boosted, replied to and pushed as
+  notifications exactly like a person's messages are.
+- They get an avatar (initials until one is uploaded) served by the same `Users::AvatarsController`.
+
+Two extra columns make a bot a bot:
+
+- `bot_code` is the credential a bot authenticates its requests with. It's generated with
+  `SecureRandom` when the bot is created and is only ever shown on the bots page.
+- `creator_id` is the person who set the bot up, which is how "your bots" is scoped. A bot
+  never signs in, so it's also given a password digest nobody knows to satisfy the
+  database's `NOT NULL` constraint.
+
+### What a bot request looks like
+
+The bot API skips the checks a browser request needs: there's no session to sign in with, no
+CSRF token to present, and no modern browser to report. It's authenticated by the bot code
+alone.
+
+### Where bots are hidden
+
+Bots are room members, not people, so they're kept out of the account's user lists with
+`User.without_bots` (the admin pages). They're deliberately kept in the room member pickers,
+so that they can be added to rooms the same way people are.
